    "physical_description": null,
    "summary": "Begun at the end of the reign of Shah Ismail (reigned 1501–24), first king of the Safavid dynasty of Iran, this copy of the <em>Shah-nama</em> (Book of Kings) was completed during the reign of his son Shah Tahmasp. Unparalleled in scope and refinement, the book included 259 paintings by master artists in the royal workshop. Two calligraphers copied the text in an elegant, flowing <em>nasta‘liq</em> script. In 1568, Shah Tahmasp gave this book as a gift to the Ottoman sultan Selim II of Turkey.",
